on, Kenya, between villages with 32 and without rice irrigation.\n[62, PDF page 3] They found that villages with rice irrigation had a e 30–300 times higher prevalence of the 27,28 local malaria vector, yet paradoxically, a lower prevalence of malaria (0–9% compared with 17–54%).\n[63, PDF page 3] One potential explanation for this so ecalled “paddies 29 Incorporating proe eparadox” was that households engaged ein irrigated agriculture had higher in ee comes and were thus more able to pay for malaria treatment and bednets.\n[64, PDF page 3] But although average cash income in the villages using irrigatation was higher, poverty was rife in all villages and there 3 was no evidence that people in the vil ee lages with irrigation made greater efforts to protect themselves.\n[65, PDF page 3] The suggested alee ternative explanation came from another agriculture–environment component: that in the irrigated villages, mosquitoes were more likely to feed off the cattle kept for economic reasons, thus diverting\n[66, PDF page 4] 987Bulletin of the World Health Organization | December 2006, 84 (12) Links between agriculture and health Corinna Hawkes et al. them from humans as a source of bloode meal and reducing disease transmission.\n[67, PDF page 4] In the other direction, malaria afe e fects agricultural producers by reducing their ability to work.\n[68, PDF page 4] A study in an area of intensive vegetable farming in Côte d’Ivoire showed that malaria led to abee sences from work of between 0–26 days in a 10emonth period; this in turn was directly correlated with reduced overall yields and revenues.33 The links between malaria and ag ee riculture present opportunities for in ee novative approaches to address poor health and livelihoods.\n[69, PDF page 4] 34 Management of agricultural water has been shown to reduce morbidity and mortality from malaria.35 In a case reported from Sichuan province, China, a shift in ire e rigation techniques to an annual cycle of wet crop/dry crop rotation resulted in a reduction of vector breeding to a level lower than that required to sustain malaria transmission.36 Other potential approaches include keeping cattle as deliberate bait, and combining health inee terventions (i.e. distribution of bednets) with irrigation programmes.","source_validation":{"candidate_id":"candidate-cc39b97fe9f1320104cbc284ce72ea2b","decision":"entailed","model_decision":"entailed","project_scope":{"decision":"in_scope","rationale":"Rice irrigation practice linked to malaria vector prevalence, an agriculture-environment vector/plant-pathogen pathway segment."},"model_matches":{"relation_match":true,"direction_match":true,"certainty_match":true},"rationale":"Evidence states villages with rice irrigation had 30–300 times higher prevalence of the local malaria vector than villages without rice irrigation; associative descriptive claim matches.","validation_policy":"source-fidelity-and-project-scope-v5","abbreviation_review":{"status":"clear","corrections":[],"unresolved":[]},"claim_text_match":true,"occurrence_provenance_fields":[],"entity_alignment":{"subject":{"mention":"rice irrigation","kind":"same_concept","explanation":"Exact surface mention in evidence.","evidence":"rice irrigation","canonical":"rice irrigation"},"object":{"mention":"local malaria vector","kind":"same_concept","explanation":"Exact surface mention in evidence.","evidence":"local malaria vector","canonical":"local malaria vector"}},"proof":{"subject":"rice irrigation","relation":"30–300 times higher prevalence of the","object":"local malaria vector"},"input_signature":"4af50171c3bce3d41f6893ca7eecdfa6891db2cf532b0e31d446c5efcfe1be5f"}},"validation":null,"source_review":{"input_signature":"fb0b393fb30a99ba62633546e58663464626e348932d55e4f1aaaaf59b14ec44","revision":"47967ff457f86c5074b4e7f81aed308e7dc8c194973e7691cac835960ae11fff","active_reviews":{},"stale_dimensions":[],"fidelity_decision":"entailed","scope_decision":"in_scope","can_accept":true},"source_r