r beyond local comee munities.14 This has led foodeimporting countries to define regulatory standards; if levels of contaminants exceed the standard, they will not be imported.\n[73, PDF page 4] Such standards have implications for the livelihoods of agricultural communities in exporting countries.\n[74, PDF page 4] Otsuki et al. used a model to compare the impact of a regulatory standard proposed by the European Union in 1997 on African food exports, with the impact of the inee ternational standard.43 They concluded that the regulation would have led to declines in African exports of cereals, dried fruits and nuts worth US$ 670 million in exchange for one or two lives saved in the EU.\n[75, PDF page 4] This result implies that a balanced tradeeoff is needed between the direct health risks posed by aflatoxins and the indirect health risks presented by potentially lowered incomes in agriculee tural communities.\n[76, PDF page 4] But again, when the interactive naee ture of the problem is taken into account, relatively simple solutions to these risks to health and livelihoods can be identiee fied.\n[77, PDF page 4] For example, a recent study showed that lowecost agricultural interventions such as using wooden pallets for crop storage reduced exposure to aflatoxins in local communities in West Africa by more than half.44","source_validation":{"candidate_id":"candidate-be2c15dd450cc5e9341fe68eeadd47ad","decision":"entailed","model_decision":"entailed","project_scope":{"decision":"in_scope","rationale":"Aflatoxin consumption associated with child underweight is a food-contaminant-to-human-health outcome within scope."},"model_matches":{"relation_match":true,"direction_match":true,"certainty_match":true},"rationale":"Source states regular consumption even of low levels is associated with underweight among children; associative certainty and child population match.","validation_policy":"source-fidelity-and-project-scope-v5","abbreviation_review":{"status":"clear","correct